PrismRTG - RTG for AmigaOS 3.x
=======================

A from-scratch retargetable-graphics system for two boards:
  * Village Tronic Picasso II / GBAPII++ (Cirrus GD542x), Zorro II
  * Piccolo, Piccolo SD64, GVP Spectrum 28/24, Ateo Graffity, Picasso IV
    (Zorro III mode) - emulator-tested only
  * MNT ZZ9000, Zorro III
  * Z3660 accelerator's RTG output (A4000)

It takes the place of Picasso96: the two cannot both be active on one
Amiga, and PrismRTG does not start while Picasso96's monitor files are in
DEVS:Monitors. Needs Kickstart 3.1+ and a 68020 or better (no FPU needed).
The driver takes about 335 KB of fast RAM (none of chip RAM); a Workbench
at 800x600 16-bit with MUI and a network stack runs in 2 MB chip + 2 MB
fast, and 4 MB fast leaves room for the usual programs.

Install:   double-click Install_PrismRTG (the standard Installer), or from a
           Shell in this drawer:  Installer Install_PrismRTG MINUSER AVERAGE
           It finds the card, sets the Workbench screen and, if Picasso96
           is installed, lists its monitor files and asks: back them up
           (moved to SYS:Storage/Picasso96-parked, never deleted) or leave
           them (then PrismRTG stays off). Remove moves them back. With
           Picasso96's settings on the Amiga it offers to switch on the
           same screen sizes (C:PrismImport; Picasso96's file is only read).
Update, install afresh (new settings) or remove: run Install_PrismRTG again.

What goes where
  C:PrismD               the driver; started at boot
  LIBS:Prism/#?.driver   one module per kind of board; PrismD loads the
                         one it needs. A driver for another board is one
                         more file here (docs/writing-a-driver.md on
                         GitHub); BOARD=<name> selects it, Auto tries it
  C:PrismSetup           the installer's helper (card detection)
  C:PrismProbe           lists the expansion boards PrismRTG can drive
  C:PrismImport          Picasso96's screen sizes into PrismRTG's settings
  DEVS:Monitors/Prism    starts PrismD before Workbench opens
  SYS:Prefs/PrismPrefs   modes, refresh rates, board, blitter, palette, log
  ENVARC:Prism.prefs     the settings

Using it
  Reboot, open Prefs/ScreenMode and choose a PRISM: mode (8bit or 16bit).
  Programs that ask for CyberGraphX or for the Picasso96 API get PrismRTG's
  own built-in cybergraphics.library and Picasso96API.library.

New in 1.1 beta 7
  The Z3660's RTG output, by Jusii: a driver for the Z3660 accelerator's
  HDMI framebuffer (firmware 1.03 or newer, autoconfig_rtg YES), with its
  hardware pointer, the monitor switch (monitor_switch in z3660cfg.txt, or
  ENV:Z3660-MONSWITCH), and fills, copies, text, lines and planar blits on
  its blitter. Written and tested on his Z3660.
  Seven more sizes in PrismPrefs (1280x800, 1600x1200, 1920x1200,
  1680x1050, 1600x900, 1366x768, 2560x1440), off until you switch them on,
  shown where the board can (Jusii). Workbench stays on the card after a
  ScreenMode change that keeps a smaller size than the mode (Jusii). A
  Workbench size larger than its PRISM mode is not opened on the card: OS
  3.2 cuts it to the mode's size, OS 3.1 falls back to a chipset Workbench -
  set the size to the mode's own there.

New in 1.1 beta 6 (tested in emulators only - please report what you see)
  Two graphics boards at once, each with its own monitor (PRISM: and
  PRISM2:) and front screen. Screen mode promotion: programs that open a
  chipset screen get it on the board (PrismPrefs "Promote screens", with a
  per-program list). Four sizes of your own in the mode list, with VESA
  timings computed on the Picasso II family. Video layers for players
  (cgxvideo.library and the Picasso96 PIP with YUV formats); on a ZZ9000
  with the BlitterStudio firmware 2.8+ the board shows them itself. Gamma
  for 256-colour screens. Oversized screens with autoscroll. Pointers
  bigger than the board's sprite. Update check over HTTPS in PrismPrefs
  (needs AmiSSL 5). The installer updates, installs afresh or removes.
  PrismPrefs and PrismSetup can be translated (no translations yet; see
  docs/translating.md on GitHub). This ReadMe also comes as PrismRTG.guide.
  Faster on a 68030 than beta 5: fills +11-12%, ReadPixel +95%, lines +23%.
  Fixed: the pointer stopping short on screens wider than 1280 (#1),
  dragging, the software pointer under drawing, flicker of a video layer
  in a blitted band, and many smaller things from two code reviews.

New in 1.1 beta 5
  From the first reports on hardware I don't have - an Apollo Vampire
  through its P96 driver, and a MiSTer's ZZ9000 core. Thank you.
  Dragged Workbench icons are shown again (they were drawn where nothing
  could see them). On the P96 driver board, copies from an off-screen
  bitmap to a window use the card's blitter: browsers scroll three to six
  times faster in WinUAE; the same on UAE (native). A card whose own
  planar blit or text blit gets a test picture wrong is drawn on the CPU
  instead (the Vampire's planar blit at 16 bits; the MiSTer's text) - the
  log says so. The pointer no longer lags when the disk is busy.
  New sizes 960x540 and 1920x1080 where the board can show them.
  PrismPrefs has Drag screens and Soft pointer switches. While dragging, a
  screen of another size behind the dragged one stays black (the card
  shows one mode at a time). Small fills, copies and text 1-3% faster on a
  68030. PrismCheck reports how the board is driven.

New in 1.1 beta 4 "Tequila" - please update from beta 3
  Fixes a crash beta 3 could hit: drawing text used almost 2 KB of the
  calling program's stack (Workbench, IPrefs - often 4 KB in all).
  Thirteen bugs from a code review fixed: among them ScrollRaster in a
  partly covered window, a pointer that could be copied with the screen,
  a stale palette cache, and quitting PrismD while programs still draw.
  Faster: pixels, lines and small blits 3-11%; text on the native UAE
  driver five times; and dragging a screen no longer eats the machine -
  the compositor redoes only what changed and copies the dragged screen
  on the card's blitter. 35 KB less fast RAM.
  New board: Ateo Concepts Graffity (Zorro II and III), emulation only.
  A driver for a new board is one file: src/drv_template.c, built with
  tools/mkdriver.sh, loaded by name or found by itself in LIBS:Prism.
  docs/writing-a-driver.md and docs/driver-development.md on GitHub.
  Tested on every board WinUAE emulates: docs/emulated-cards.md.

New in 1.1 beta 3
  Needs Kickstart 3.1 or newer (3.0 never worked; PrismD now says so).
  The board drivers are separate modules in LIBS:Prism, drawing goes
  through format-aware operations a driver can decline or fail safely,
  and Picasso96 card drivers can bring their own memory handling - all
  Stefan Reinauer's work, with speed and robustness changes on top.
  Software pointer for boards without a working hardware pointer.

New in 1.1 beta (experimental - please report how it goes)
  Two more boards, chosen by hand: Board in PrismPrefs, or BOARD= in
  ENVARC:Prism.prefs. Neither is picked automatically.
  "UAE (native)"  BOARD=UAEGFX - UAE's own RTG card in WinUAE/Amiberry,
                  without Picasso96.
  "P96 driver"    BOARD=P96 P96CARD=<file> - drives a card through its
                  existing Picasso96 card driver (.card), without the rest
                  of Picasso96. Tested with UAE's uaegfx.card; reported
                  working on an Apollo Vampire with vampiregfx.card.
  Details: docs/uaegfx.md and docs/p96-drivers.md on GitHub.

Two boards (new in 1.1 beta 6 - tested in an emulator so far)
  With two boards in one machine - a ZZ9000 and a GBAPII++ or Picasso II -
  each on its own monitor, PrismRTG drives both. The first board's modes
  are PRISM:..., the second's PRISM2:...; pick a board by picking a mode
  (ScreenMode, a program's mode requester). Each board shows its own front
  screen, and the pointer is on the board of the screen in front.
  Any two of the supported cards work, two of the same kind too. Either one
  can be the primary (PRISM: modes, so Workbench): Board "Auto, swapped" in
  PrismPrefs (PRIMARY=SECOND) makes the second board found the primary - say,
  the card on the bigger monitor; the status line names the boards and their
  expansion addresses. "Auto, 1 board" (SECONDBOARD=OFF) keeps to one board;
  with two, the Test button asks which board shows the test picture.

Software pointer
  Boards without a working hardware pointer get a pointer drawn into the
  screen; Soft pointer in PrismPrefs (SOFTWAREPOINTER=ON in the prefs
  file) forces it, e.g. on a MiSTer.
  It costs a few percent of drawing speed.

Screen mode promotion
  "Promote screens" in PrismPrefs (PROMOTE=ON) opens a screen a program
  asks for on PAL, NTSC or another chipset monitor on the board instead,
  in the smallest 256-colour mode that holds it, at the depth asked for.
  The Programs list makes exceptions: a program kept on the chipset with
  the switch on, or promoted with it off (KEEPPROG= / PROMOTEPROG=, by
  command name or task name). The screen is the mode's size, so a 640x256
  screen drawn edge to edge leaves a band; programs with their own bitmap
  or more than 8 planes are left alone. PrismD reads this when it starts.

Video layers (overlay)
  PrismD carries its own cgxvideo.library and the Picasso96 PIP calls
  take the YUV formats, so video players (FroggerNG with DISPLAY=VLAYER
  or DISPLAY=P96, and others that use either) show their picture in a
  window. The card has no overlay engine, so the frames are converted
  and scaled by the CPU: small windows on a 68030, full ones on an 060.

Large pointers
  A pointer bigger than the board's sprite (the ZZ9000's is 32x48) is
  drawn by the software pointer, whole, instead of being cut.

Oversized screens
  A screen opened with autoscroll and a size beyond its mode (ScreenMode's
  "Autoscroll" with a bigger size, or a program's own) is scrolled by
  Intuition when the pointer pushes an edge, on the Picasso II family and
  the ZZ9000.

Gamma
  "Gamma" in PrismPrefs (GAMMA=1.4 in the prefs file): the palette's
  curve for 256-colour screens and the Test picture. Deeper screens are
  not touched (their colours are in the pixels, not the DAC).

Your own sizes
  The mode list ends with four "custom" entries: pick one, type a width
  (a multiple of 8) and a height, Set size, and Test shows it at once.
  PrismD offers it after a restart at every depth the board manages.
  The Picasso II family computes a VESA timing for any size within its
  pixel clock (1152x864, 1360x768 ...); the ZZ9000 shows the sizes its
  firmware has (1600x1200, 720x576, 2560x1440 ...). In the prefs file:
  CUSTOM1=1152x864 .. CUSTOM4=.

Update check
  "Updates" in PrismPrefs (GitHub, a mirror, or GitHub then the mirror)
  and "Check now": C:PrismUpdate asks for the newest release over HTTPS
  and offers to download and install it (the installer then runs as
  usual, and the update takes effect at the next reboot). Needs a
  running TCP/IP stack and AmiSSL 5, or an SSL offload with the AmiSSL
  API. A mirror is MIRROR=https://host/dir/ in the prefs file, a
  directory with latest.txt ("version archive"). From a Shell:
  PrismUpdate, PrismUpdate GET.

Monitor power saving
  "Sleep" in PrismPrefs (POWERSAVE=10 in the prefs file): after that many
  minutes without a key or mouse event the board's picture goes dark, and
  the next key or mouse move brings it back. 0, the default, is never.
  The Picasso II family turns its picture and syncs off, so the monitor
  sleeps; on the ZZ9000, Picasso IV, UAE and P96 cards the screen goes
  black. Use and Save in PrismPrefs take effect at once.

ARexx
  PrismD has an ARexx port, PRISM (RexxMast must run): VERSION, BOARDS,
  BOARD, VRAM, MODES, MODE <n>, MODEID <w> <h> <bits>, TEST <w> <h> <bits>
  [hz] and TESTEND (the Test picture), POWERSAVE [min], SLEEP, WAKE, DARK,
  LOG [ON|OFF], HELP. docs/arexx.md on GitHub has the details.

Picasso96's sizes
  C:PrismImport reads DEVS:Picasso96Settings and switches on every size
  and depth that was on there; sizes PrismRTG lacks become custom sizes
  (up to four). PrismImport TEST shows what it would do; ONLY switches
  every other mode off. PrismD takes the list at its next start.

Dragging and PIP windows
  Vertical dragging between RTG screens (DRAGGING=ON) and P96 memory-window
  PIPs go through a compositor that redoes only what changed and copies the
  dragged screen with the card's blitter; drawing on a dragged screen keeps
  most of its speed on a 68030. Switched off (dragging's default) they
  cost nothing. Few programs have used it yet - please report.

24-bit or 32-bit?
  Both are true colour. On the GBAPII++ the card's blitter can draw text
  and fills at 32-bit but not at 24-bit (text is several times faster),
  while copying and scrolling are about a quarter faster at 24-bit because
  each pixel is smaller. 24-bit is 640x480 only there; 32-bit also does
  800x600. The Picasso II has 24-bit only, the ZZ9000 32-bit only.

If something goes wrong
  Hold the LEFT MOUSE BUTTON while booting: PrismRTG doesn't start and
  Workbench opens on a native mode. Then use PrismPrefs (switch a mode
  off, change its refresh, or turn the blitter off) and reboot.
  With Log "On" in PrismPrefs, PrismD writes T:PrismD.log. "Sync" writes
  each line to SYS:PrismD.log at once - slow, but it is there after a
  machine that stops while starting is reset.
  256-colour screens with red and blue exchanged (a real Piccolo or
  Spectrum may do this): tick "Swap red/blue" in PrismPrefs.

Reporting a problem
  Download PrismCheck from the same release page, run it, and attach
  the report it writes to an issue at
  https://github.com/lainejones/PrismRTG/issues

